\image(figures/floyd_warshall_controls.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Dense and disconnected controls count actual finite additions. The vertical scale is explicitly log2; the table gives the untransformed counts. Validation and capture controls execute in the same complete program.)
\alttext(A native line plot shows log-base-two addition counts across vertex counts one to thirty-two, with dense counts rising three units per doubling and disconnected counts rising one.)

Reconstruction validates both full square matrices, all finite-or-positive-infinity
costs and every hop ID before shortcuts. An affected pair throws a domain error;
unreachable or broken sentinel chains return empty, while an unaffected diagonal
returns its single vertex. External cyclic chains throw after at most n moves.
Shape/value validation protects access but does not prove that externally supplied
matrices contain optimal costs. Validation makes reconstruction O(n squared+path
length), with O(path length) returned storage.

Input edges must have valid endpoints and finite weights. Every attempted finite
candidate addition must remain finite, including a candidate that would not improve
a route. Checked counters and matrix/container bounds precede allocations; overflow
is reported rather than silently turning a reachable route into the infinity sentinel.

For full history, define A=2n+2n squared. The complete upper entry bound is

$$
C=E+A+(n+1)(1+A)+n^3.
$$
\alttext(The complete trace bound counts E edge tuples, A final-matrix row and cell entries, n plus one stage records with their A matrix entries, and at most n cubed improvement records.)

The units are owned tuples, outer-row entries, cells, stage objects and improvement
records, not bytes or peak RAM. The conservative bound is checked before copies or
execution, with a default one-million-entry cap. Zero disables only that optional
cap. Arithmetic/container limits remain, and a graph with few improvements can still
need large full-stage storage. No replay, truncation or partial trace is returned.
Inputs and captured matrices/edges are independent owned values.

The standard all-pairs scope and negative-cycle return convention are also described
in the official
[Boost.Graph documentation](https://www.boost.org/doc/libs/latest/libs/graph/doc/html/graph/algorithms/shortest_paths/floyd_warshall_shortest.html).
The present API adds its own validated matrix/path contracts and actual evidence;
this report makes no claim of cross-library floating-point or performance equivalence.
All five complete editable programs use native APIs and deterministic graph fixtures,
with no external dataset. Independent edge-list Bellman–Ford tests check finite and
unbounded pairs; frozen records preserve matrices, next hops, valid routes, stage
boundaries and strict updates, alongside address/undefined-behavior sanitizers.
